<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wehrmacht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wehrmacht was the unified armed forces of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1935 to 1945, comprising the army, navy and air force. Built up rapidly in defiance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, it won swift victories early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> before being worn down on the Eastern Front and defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. Its units took part in war crimes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, and it surrendered unconditionally on 8 May 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wehrmacht.jpg" alt="Wehrmacht" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balkenkreuz.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Wehrmacht, from wehren, to defend, and Macht, power, had long been used for any nation's armed forces. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> limited the army to one hundred thousand men and the navy to fifteen thousand, with at most six battleships, six cruisers and twelve destroyers, and it forbade submarines, tanks, heavy artillery and an air force. A new force, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a>, was established on 23 March 1921.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> died on 2 August 1934, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became commander in chief. In that month the entire military took the Hitler oath, a pledge of personal obedience, on the initiative of Defence Minister Werner von Blomberg and General Walther von Reichenau. Hitler was surprised at the offer, and the view that he imposed the oath is false. In 1935 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a> was replaced by the Wehrmacht, made up of the Heer, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kriegsmarine">Kriegsmarine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>, and conscription returned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hitler made himself Supreme Commander of the Armed Forces. After the Blomberg-Fritsch Affair he abolished the Ministry of War and put the Wehrmacht High Command, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberkommando-der-wehrmacht">Oberkommando der Wehrmacht</a>, under Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel in its place. Early campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and elsewhere relied on combined arms tactics of aircraft, tanks and infantry, which foreign journalists called Blitzkrieg. The advance into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> brought its first major defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1941, and by late 1942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was losing the initiative in all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>.</p><p>Resistance within the forces began with the Oster conspiracy of 1938. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> trust in Hitler's leadership waned, and on 20 July 1944 a group of officers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claus-von-stauffenberg">Claus von Stauffenberg</a> attempted to assassinate him. The attempt failed, 4,980 people were executed, and the military salute was replaced by the Hitler salute. The Wehrmacht's unconditional surrender took effect on 8 May 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>German forces, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a> and collaborator units, lost about 5,318,000 men killed, dead in custody or missing. The Wehrmacht cooperated closely with the SS and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsatzgruppen">Einsatzgruppen</a> and committed war crimes, chiefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Of 3.2 million Soviet prisoners taken between the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> and the following spring, 2.8 million died in German hands.</p><p>After the war former officers and veterans' groups promoted the myth of a clean Wehrmacht, an apolitical force innocent of the regime's crimes. German historians began to challenge this view in the late 1970s and the 1980s. Historians have also reassessed the Wehrmacht's fighting power, noting that it often inflicted heavier losses than it took while outnumbered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wehrmacht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Wehrmacht</b> never reached the South Caucasus, but Armenia supplied food, manpower and war material in the Great Patriotic War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nazi regime annexed it in all but name under the Gauleiter Gustav Simon, imposed German law and in 1942 introduced conscription of young Luxembourgers into the <b>Wehrmacht</b>, which provoked a general strike that was brutally repressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Army of the Independent State of Croatia, the Home Guard, was founded in April 1941 with the <b>Wehrmacht</b>'s consent, beside the Ustaše Militia and a gendarmerie.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1941 Hungary let the <b>Wehrmacht</b> cross its territory for the invasion of Yugoslavia, joined the operations and annexed the Bačka region of Vojvodina and Muraköz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Conscription was reintroduced in March 1935 in defiance of the Treaty of Versailles, the Reichswehr became the <b>Wehrmacht</b>, the Luftwaffe was unveiled and troops reoccupied the demilitarised Rhineland in March 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1943 the Italian Army occupied Monaco and formed a fascist administration; in September, after Mussolini's fall, the <b>Wehrmacht</b> took over and the deportation of the Jews began.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Military</a></li></ul></section>
